WHY THIS WORKS
- acknowledges the years of dismissal without turning it into a speech about it
- is clear that coaching does not remove tissue, while still offering real value
- arc 2 stays firmly out of a surgical decision

CONVERSATION 1: stage 3, one year trying since her lap

Lead: I have stage 3 endo, had a laparoscopy 14 months ago and we've been trying since. Still nothing. I'm starting to think it's never going to happen.

Sonia: Fourteen months post-surgery with nothing is discouraging, particularly when the surgery was supposed to be the reset.

Endo makes conception harder in more than one way at once, and the tissue that was removed is only one of them.

Was anything looked at afterwards, or was the plan just to try?

Lead: Just to try. They said the window after surgery is the best chance so we should get on with it.

Sonia: That window is real. Using it that way is narrow though, because it treats the surgery as the whole intervention.

How have your symptoms been since? Pain, cycles, fatigue, gut.

Lead: Pain is better than it was. But my digestion is a mess and I'm tired constantly.

Sonia: Both of those are common with endo and both are things I'd want to work on, not accept as background noise.

Inflammation doesn't stay in one area. It shows up in the gut, in energy, and in the environment an embryo has to implant into.

Lead: Nobody has connected any of that to the fertility side for me.

Sonia: It rarely gets connected, because the surgical and the fertility conversations happen in different rooms.

I want to be honest about what I can and can't do. I can't remove endometriosis and I'm not going to pretend coaching does that. What I can do is work on the environment around it, which is where most of the remaining room usually is.

Lead: That's more realistic than what I've been told, which is basically IVF or nothing.

Sonia: IVF may well end up being right for you and I'd never talk anyone out of it. I'd just want the body it's happening in to be in the best shape it can be first.

Where does having a baby sit for you at the moment? Is it the thing everything else is arranged around?

Lead: It's the only thing.

Sonia: Then let's treat it that way. A call with my team covers the surgery, the symptoms and the fourteen months since. It costs money, and I'd rather say that now than on the call.

Lead: Understood. I'd like to book.

Sonia: Here's the link.

